<h1 class = "catalog-desc-header">Save File Location</h1>

<div style = "display: flex; justify-content: space-between; margin-bottom: 15vh;"> 
    <div style = "flex: 2;">
        <section class = "catalog-desc-container">
            <h2 class = "catalog-desc-subheader">On Windows: </h3>
            <code class = "catalog-desc-file-location">
                C:\Users\[YourUsername]\AppData\Local\UNDERTALE
            </code>
            <br><br>
            <code class = "catalog-desc-file-location">
                You can also paste into the file explorer search bar: <br><br>%localappdata%\UNDERTALE
            </code>
        </section>

        <section class = "catalog-desc-container">
            <h2 class = "catalog-desc-subheader">On macOS: </h3>
            <code class = "catalog-desc-file-location">
                /Users/[username]/Library/Application Support/com.tobyfox.undertale
            </code>
        </section>

        <section class = "catalog-desc-container">
            <h2 class = "catalog-desc-subheader">On Linux: </h3>
            <code class = "catalog-desc-file-location">
                /home/[username]/.config/UNDERTALE
            </code>
        </section>

        <p class = "catalog-desc-text"> 
            After locating the save folder, there are 3 primary save files.
        </p>
        <ul class = "catalog-desc-text">
            <li><code class = "catalog-desc-text">file0:</code> Main save file used when the game loads from a SAVE point</li>
            <li><code class = "catalog-desc-text">file9:</code> Hidden/autosave-style state and game-over reload behavior</li>
            <li><code class = "catalog-desc-text">undertale.ini:</code> Affects menu-displayed items. Does not affect gameplay</li>
        </ul>
        <p class = "catalog-desc-text"> 
            Drag <code class = "catalog-desc-text">file0</code> into our save file display tool since its the primary file
            that you can "safely" manipulate. See more details below.
        </p>

    </div>

    <section style = "flex: 1; display: flex; justify-content: center; align-items: center;">
        <img class = "catalog-desc-img" src = "/Photos/Undertale-save-file-structure.png" style = "flex: 1">
    </section>

</div>

<h1 class = "catalog-desc-header">Encoding Algorithm Summary</h1>
<p class = "catalog-desc-text">
    Undertale doesn't use any algorithm to encode their files but structures their save by "lines". When you open <code class = "catalog-desc-text">file0</code>,
    you'll see numbers organized in separeted lines. Steam user <em>Maligned Gray Freak</em> researched each lines'
    corresponding values as seen <a href = "https://steamcommunity.com/sharedfiles/filedetails/?id=1821253151">here</a>.
    In summary, the structure of the save file is...
</p>
<p class = "catalog-desc-text">
    <i class="fa-solid fa-arrow-right"></i> Line 1: {your_save_name} => "Your save name" <br>
    <i class="fa-solid fa-arrow-right"></i> Line 2: {Some #} => "Your LV Level" <br>
    <i class="fa-solid fa-arrow-right"></i> Line 3: {Some #} => "Your max HP" <br>
    <i class="fa-solid fa-arrow-right"></i> ...And so on <br>
</p>

<p class = "catalog-desc-text">
    <strong>Important:</strong> Our save file decoder displays ALL the lines, but only KNOWN values are named.
    For example, line 10 of <code class = "catalog-desc-text">file0</code> is named "Line 10"
    with its corresponding value. This is because we werent able to figure out what ALL lines mean,
    only the ones that Maligned Gray Freak were able to find. <strong>CHANGE THESE AT YOUR OWN RISK!</strong> 
    (Make a backup at least)
</p>

<p class = "catalog-desc-text" >
    Here is the summary of how we decoded it:
    <ol class = "catalog-desc-text" style = "margin-bottom: 15vh;">
        <li>Take raw save file</li>
        <li>Create a list of known pairings (i.e. Line 1 = Player name, etc)</li>
        <li>Loop through each line (Each line is separated by a "\r\n")</li>
        <li>Then store each value into a JSON</li>
        <li>If a specific line of <code class = "catalog-desc-text">file0</code> is known, make the JSON element's key name its corresponding name</li>
        <li>Otherwise, make it "Line + ${the_current_line_we_are_on_during_iteration}"</li>
        <li>Then display the whole JSON to the user</li>
    </ol>
</p>
        